Q: Queue is often stuck; after deleting records and re-inserting data it processes again; after some days same issue. Why?

One line answer:
Usually unacked / poison messages or bad DB-linked data blocks consumers — delete+reinsert “resets” state so work flows again; root cause is still in ACK, retry, DLQ, or data handling.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ What “stuck” usually means in RabbitMQ UI

Open Management UI → Queues → your queue. Check:

| Column / metric     | Meaning                                      | Red flag                          |
| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Ready               | Waiting to be delivered                      | Growing forever                   |
| Unacked             | Given to consumer, waiting ACK               | High / never goes to 0            |
| Consumers           | Active listeners                             | 0 = nobody processing             |
| Message rates       | publish / deliver / ack                      | deliver without ack = hang        |

If Unacked stays high → consumer took messages but did not ACK (crash, hang, bug).
If Ready grows and Consumers = 0 → consumer down.
If Ready grows and Consumers > 0 but rate ~0 → consumer blocked / slow / failing loop.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Why delete records + re-insert “fixes” it (important clue)

That pattern usually means processing depends on DB records, not only on the queue.

Common stories:
1) Consumer reads message → loads DB row → row in bad state → throws / hangs
2) Infinite retry of same bad message(s) → queue looks stuck
3) Delete rows + insert fresh data → publisher sends new messages OR consumer can succeed again
4) Days later bad/corrupt/locked/stale data returns → same symptom

So: RabbitMQ is often the symptom; bad message + bad data + missing DLQ is the disease.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Top root causes (check in this order)

1) Unacked messages (most common)
- Manual ACK, but exception path never acks/nacks
- Consumer thread blocked (DB lock, HTTP timeout, deadlock)
- Prefetch too high → one slow consumer holds many messages

2) Poison message (bad payload) without DLQ
- Same message fails forever → requeue → stuck loop
- Fix: retry limit + Dead Letter Queue

3) Consumer crash / connection half-dead
- Process alive but listener stuck
- TCP connection open, no progress

4) DB-related hang (matches delete/reinsert)
- Row lock / long transaction
- Unique constraint / duplicate processing
- Status field never moves (e.g. stuck in PROCESSING)
- Consumer waits on missing FK / null required field

5) Single active consumer / exclusive consumer
- One stuck consumer owns the queue; others idle

6) Publisher confirms / consumer ACK mismatch
- App thinks work done, broker still waiting ack (or opposite)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ What to check RIGHT NOW (colleague checklist)

A) RabbitMQ Management
1. Ready vs Unacked counts
2. Consumer count
3. Message rates (deliver vs ack)
4. Get messages (if safe) — inspect payload of stuck ones

B) Application logs
1. Exceptions on @RabbitListener
2. Threads blocked on DB / HTTP
3. Same message id repeating forever

C) DB
1. Rows stuck in PROCESSING / PENDING forever
2. Locks: long running transactions
3. After “delete + reinsert” — which status/fields change?

D) Code
1. AcknowledgeMode: MANUAL or AUTO?
2. On exception: nack(requeue=true) forever? → poison loop
3. Prefetch setting
4. Idempotency: reprocessing same record safe?

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Healthy consumer pattern (Spring AMQP idea)

@RabbitListener(queues = "job-queue")
public void handle(JobMessage msg, Channel channel,
                   @Header(AmqpHeaders.DELIVERY_TAG) long tag) throws IOException {
    try {
        process(msg);                 // may touch DB
        channel.basicAck(tag, false); // success
    } catch (RetryableException e) {
        channel.basicNack(tag, false, true);  // requeue carefully / better: retry + DLQ
    } catch (Exception e) {
        channel.basicNack(tag, false, false); // do NOT requeue forever → send to DLQ setup
    }
}

Better than endless requeue:
- retry 3 times (Spring Retry / x-death)
- then Dead Letter Queue
- alert on DLQ depth

Prefetch example:
factory.setPrefetchCount(1); // heavy jobs: don’t hoard

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Why it comes back “after some days”

Because the trigger returns:
- bad/partial records accumulate again
- one poison message lands again
- DB locks / load grow over time
- memory / connection leak → consumer freezes
- disk alarm / flow control on broker (less common but possible)

Delete+reinsert is a temporary reset, not a fix.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Real fix plan

1) Identify: Unacked vs Ready vs no consumers
2) Fix ACK paths (always ack/nack; never swallow exception silently)
3) Add DLQ + max retry (stop poison loops)
4) Fix DB status machine (don’t leave rows stuck in PROCESSING)
5) Make consumer idempotent
6) Lower prefetch for heavy jobs
7) Monitor: Unacked, Ready, DLQ depth, consumer count, listener errors
8) Stop using “purge queue / delete all rows” as the recovery procedure

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ 30-second answer (interview / team discussion)

“If a RabbitMQ queue looks stuck but works after deleting and reinserting DB data, I check Ready vs Unacked first. Usually consumers hold unacked messages or keep retrying poison messages tied to bad records. Delete-reinsert resets data so processing succeeds again. The real fix is correct ACK/NACK, retry limits with DLQ, and fixing the DB/business state that makes certain messages fail.”
